rom 4 to Q. Fortunately, I hold pairs of 8, 9, and J, which means I have "spare" singles of these ranks. Playing the ♠7 would break my straight, which is detrimental. Playing a high card like an Ace or 2 is unnecessary force against a 6. The most efficient move is to play my lowest "spare" card, which is an 8. This beats the Landlord\'s 6, advances my hand by clearing a card, and preserves my straight and high-value control cards (A, 2, BJ) for crucial moments.\n\nReason: I have a pair of 8s, so playing one 8 allows me to keep the other 8 for my 4-Q straight. This is the lowest card I can play that beats the 6 without breaking my best combination.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♦8"]}'}
